Welcome to notebook four. The last notebook gave you verbs. This one gives you the data shapes those verbs operate on. By the end of this session you will know the five collections you reach for daily in Scala. List, Vector, Set, Map, and Array. You will know what each is good at, and you will know how to pick between them.

The next notebook, notebook five, will then put the higher order operations like map, filter, and fold to work on these shapes. This notebook is about the shapes themselves, not the operations.

Let us start with the landscape in one breath. Scala ships two parallel collection hierarchies. One immutable, one mutable. They live in two different packages. The package scala dot collection dot immutable, and the package scala dot collection dot mutable. The default import that comes with every Scala program points at the immutable side. So when you write List, Vector, Set, or Map in your code without any extra import, you are getting the immutable version automatically. The mutable versions require an explicit import. This is a deliberate choice by the language designers. Immutable is the easy path. Mutable is opt in.

There is also one collection that lives outside both hierarchies. The Array. The Array is a thin wrapper over the java virtual machine's primitive array type. We will come back to Array near the end of this notebook.

One important misconception to clear up. Immutable does not mean slow. The immutable collections share structure under the hood. When you add an element to a List, the language does not copy the entire list. It creates a new cell that points at the existing tail. We will see this in the List section in a moment. The same idea applies to Vector and to Map. Producing a new collection that differs slightly from an old one is usually a small operation.

Why immutable by default though? Three reasons. The first reason is safe sharing. An immutable collection can be passed across threads, across functions, across executors in a Spark cluster, with zero coordination. No locks. No defensive copies. Two threads looking at the same value cannot disagree about what it is, because neither thread can change it. The second reason is that immutable code is easier to reason about. A value that cannot change is one less moving part. The function you read on page twelve produces the same answer whether page fourteen ran first or not. The third reason is that immutable collections share structure, so deriving a new version is cheap. Spark's entire programming model is built on this assumption. Resilient distributed data sets and data frames are immutable. Every Spark operation produces a new one. Notebook fifteen will revisit this.

Now let us meet the first collection. List. List is Scala's immutable singly linked list. It is built out of two shapes. The empty list, written as Nil, and the cons cell, written with two colons, which prepends one element to another list. So the list one two three is internally one cons two cons three cons Nil. Each cell holds a value and a pointer to the rest. You can picture it as a chain. The number one in a cell, with an arrow pointing to a cell holding two, with an arrow pointing to a cell holding three, with an arrow pointing to Nil at the end.

That layout decides what is fast and what is slow on a List. Fast operations are head, tail, isEmpty, and prepend. Calling head returns the first element. Calling tail returns the rest of the list without the first element. Both are constant time. Prepending an element using the double colon operator is also constant time, because you just create a new cell that points at the old list. Slow operations are random access by index, which is linear in the position you want, and reaching the last element, which is also linear. Anything that needs to walk to the end has to traverse the whole chain.

The takeaway for List. Reach for it when you build front to back and consume front to back, especially in code that uses recursion or pattern matching. Pattern matching on head colon colon tail is the most idiomatic way to walk a List in Scala. Use a different shape when you need indexed access or fast append.

Vector is the second collection, and it is the default indexed sequence. A Vector is internally a balanced thirty two way tree. From the outside it behaves like an array. From the inside, the tree is so shallow that indexed access at any position is effectively constant time, even for very large vectors. Practically, you can treat Vector access as constant time and not worry about it.

Vector has clean syntactic operators. The operator colon plus appends. The operator plus colon prepends. The way to remember which is which is to look at where the colon sits. The colon is always next to the collection. So vector colon plus value means append the value to the vector. Value plus colon vector means prepend the value to the vector. Both produce a new Vector and leave the original alone.

Vector also has a method called updated, which takes an index and a new value and returns a new Vector with that one position changed. The original is untouched. The new Vector reuses most of the old one internally, so this operation is cheap.

What to remember about Vector. Random access, prepend, and append are all effectively constant time. Vector is the safe default when you do not know exactly what your access pattern will be. The rule of thumb is, when in doubt between List and Vector, pick Vector. Pick List only when you specifically want cons cell ergonomics for recursion or pattern matching.

Next is Set. A Set holds unique elements with no defined iteration order. The fast operation on a Set is membership testing. Asking, is this element in the Set. That is a roughly constant time operation. Other operations follow naturally. The plus operator adds an element. The minus operator removes one. There are also set theory operators. Union combines two sets. Intersect gives you the elements in both. Diff gives you the elements in the first but not the second.

There is one quietly powerful idea hiding inside Set. A Set of type A is also a function from A to Boolean. So if you have a set of colors called s, you can call s of the string red, and it returns true or false. This is the same as calling s dot contains. Why does that matter? Because it means a Set can be passed directly anywhere a predicate is expected. You can use a Set as the argument to filter without converting it first. The higher order machinery from notebook three composes cleanly with sets.

Two things to remember about Set. The iteration order of an immutable Set is not defined. Do not write code that depends on it. And all the operators, plus, minus, union, intersect, diff, return new sets without modifying the original.

Now Map. Map is the immutable hash map. It maps keys to values, with unique keys. You construct a Map with the arrow notation. The expression alice arrow thirty produces a key value pair where alice is the key and thirty is the value. Then you wrap a series of these pairs in Map and you have a Map.

Looking up by key has two forms. The unsafe form, where you call the Map like a function with the key in parentheses, returns the value directly. If the key is missing, it throws a no such element exception. The safe form is the get method. Calling get returns an option of the value type. If the key is present, you get Some of the value. If the key is missing, you get None. Notebook nine covers Option in depth. For now, the important point is that get forces you to acknowledge missing keys at the type level instead of relying on a nullable return. There is also a method called getOrElse that returns a default value when the key is missing, without involving Option.

You modify a Map by deriving a new one. Map plus a new pair adds an entry. Map minus a key removes one. The updated method changes the value at an existing key. All of these return new maps.

One sugar to internalise. The arrow notation, like alice arrow thirty, is not a special operator built into Map. It is a method available on every value. The expression alice arrow thirty returns a tuple of String and Int. Map is just a collection of such tuples. That is why iteration over a Map yields tuples, and you can destructure them in a for expression, like for name comma age, taking pairs from the ages Map.

Now Array. Array is Scala's thin wrapper over a java array. It is mutable, fixed size, and lives outside the immutable collections hierarchy. Use Array when you need the exact memory layout that a contiguous block of primitives gives you, or when you are talking to a java application programming interface that expects an array.

Three things to register about Array. The first is that indexing uses parentheses, not square brackets. Square brackets in Scala always mean type parameters, never indices. So array of i, with parentheses, gives you the element at position i. The second is that arrays are mutable. A val binds the array, but the contents can still change in place. This is the gap that notebook two hinted at. The val locks the binding. It does not lock what the binding points to. The third is that arrays cannot grow. They have a fixed length. If you need a resizable indexed sequence, use Vector if you want immutable, or use ArrayBuffer from the mutable collections package if you want mutable.

In practice you will see Array mostly in three places. Low level performance code. java interop. And Spark internals, because Spark stores partition data as primitive arrays for cache efficiency.

Before we close, a brief stop at tuples. A tuple is not a collection in the same sense, but every Map you saw above is built on them, so it is worth meeting now. A tuple holds a small fixed number of values of possibly different types. You construct it with parentheses. The type of a two tuple of String and Int is written, open paren, String, comma, Int, close paren. You access the elements positionally with the names underscore one and underscore two. You can also destructure a tuple on the left side of a val. Writing, val open paren name comma age close paren equals pair, binds the two parts to two names at once.

Tuples are great for very small groupings. When you find yourself reaching for underscore four, or naming what each tuple field means in a comment, switch to a case class. Case classes give you named fields and the same convenience. We will meet case classes in notebook seven.

Time for a cheatsheet you can scan when you are about to declare a new variable. If you want to build front to back and walk recursively, reach for List. If you want indexed access with mixed prepend and append, reach for Vector. Vector is the default when in doubt. For membership testing or deduplication, reach for Set. For key to value lookup, reach for Map. For java interop or contiguous primitives, reach for Array. For a mutable resizable buffer, reach for mutable dot ArrayBuffer. For two or three values bundled together briefly, use a tuple. For named fields on a small record, jump ahead to case classes.

One more idea before we close. The gap between val and immutable, revisited with the example we now have. A val is a single assignment binding. It does not make what it points to immutable. If you bind a val to an Array, you cannot reassign the val to a different array, but you can still mutate the contents of the array in place. The array's first element can change from one to ninety nine while the val keeps pointing at the same array object. By contrast, if you bind a val to a List, the List has no update method, so the contents are stable too.

Two layers of guarantee are at work. The val keyword controls rebinding. Can the name point to a different value tomorrow? The collection's type controls mutation. Does the value itself have methods that change its state? Val plus an immutable collection locks both layers. That is the default you should reach for. Val plus an Array locks the binding but lets contents change underneath you. Var plus an immutable collection keeps the contents stable but lets the binding flip, which is useful in tight loops but rarely needed at higher levels.

To close, here is a tiny example that uses four collections cooperating. Imagine a list of orders, where each order is a tuple of customer name, product, and price. From that list you can derive a Set of unique customer names by mapping each order to its first element and converting to a Set. You can also derive a Map from each customer to their first order, by grouping the list by the customer field and taking the head of each group. List holds the ordered orders. Set deduplicates customer names. Map indexes by customer. Each derivation returns a new collection without modifying the previous one.

That is the shape of idiomatic Scala. You compose collections by deriving new ones. You rarely mutate in place. Notebook five will go deeper into the operations that make these derivations expressive. Map, filter, fold, groupBy, and the for yield sugar that desugars to exactly those operations.
